Important:
~~~~~~~~~~

    - Read the README file carefully; things changed.

    - NEVER RUN A TEST PROGRAM (<test>.dir/sbin/init) DIRECTLY ON YOUR
      MACHINE UNLESS YOU WROTE IT YOURSELF. PEER TESTS (all_tests, ...)
      ARE PROGRAMS WRITTEN BY OTHER PEOPLE: RUN THEM ONLY THROUGH YOUR
      KERNEL, INSIDE QEMU (make test, run_qemu, ...). A TEST THAT'S
      HARMLESS UNDER OUR KERNEL CAN DO SOMETHING VERY DIFFERENT UNDER
      LINUX; FOR EXAMPLE, A clone3 CALL OUR KERNEL REJECTS CAN BE A FORK
      UNDER LINUX, AND A LOOP OF THEM IS A FORK BOMB.
    
    - The kernel is completely yours; you can change/replace/add anything
      as long as:
           - You leave the build environment alone
           - You implement the required system calls with the correct semantics
           - The code you push is written by you + given base code on the
             master branch

Assignment:
~~~~~~~~~~~

    - Implement more system calls
    - Donate a test case
    - Answer the questions in REPORT.txt

No user/libc directory is provided. It's up to you to copy the one from
p5 and evolve it. Feel free to collaborate with others and share code
for this part.

The new system calls
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The system calls behave like Linux's: same arguments, same results, same
errors. Many are a subset of what Linux does; a call asking for a feature
your kernel doesn't support returns EINVAL.

The numbers in parentheses are the x86-64 system call numbers (RAX).

File descriptors

    - dup3 (292)
        * flags: O_CLOEXEC only
    - pipe2 (293)
        * flags: O_CLOEXEC only
        * a write to a pipe with no readers returns EPIPE; there is no
          SIGPIPE
        * a write is atomic (not mixed with other writers' bytes) only up
          to 256 bytes, not PIPE_BUF (4096)

Processes

    - getcwd (79)
    - chdir (80)

Time

    - nanosleep (35)
        * waits at least the time asked for
        * not interrupted by signals

Synchronization

    - futex (202): mutual exclusion only
        * FUTEX_WAIT (without a timeout) and FUTEX_WAKE, with or without
          FUTEX_PRIVATE_FLAG
        * Linux's other commands, FUTEX_CLOCK_REALTIME, and a timeout
          return EINVAL

Signals

    Only 5 signals: SIGINT (2), SIGUSR1 (10), SIGSEGV (11), SIGALRM (14),
    SIGTERM (15).

    - rt_sigaction (13)
        * flags: SA_SIGINFO, SA_RESTORER and SA_RESTART only (SA_RESTART
          has no effect: see below)
        * oldact is ignored: the old action isn't given back
        * sigsetsize must be 8
        * sa_mask: only the 5 signals above matter
    - rt_sigreturn (15): returns from a handler, through sa_restorer
    - kill (62)
        * pid > 0 only (no process groups, no -1)
        * sig 0 checks that the process is there
    - alarm (37): delivers SIGALRM

    Limitations:
        * a handler runs on the thread's stack (no sigaltstack, no
          SA_ONSTACK)
        * a handler runs with its signal blocked, plus sa_mask, until it
          returns; there is no sigprocmask (rt_sigprocmask)
        * a signal is taken on the way back to user mode. A system call
          that waits (read, wait4, futex, ...) isn't interrupted: the
          signal is taken when the call is done. There is no EINTR
        * a bad memory access (page fault) or a general protection fault
          (#GP) gives the faulting thread a SIGSEGV right away. If SIGSEGV
          is ignored, has no handler, or is blocked (a fault in its own
          handler), the process dies of it
        * invalid opcode (#UD) and divide error (#DE) in user mode are out
          of scope: there is no SIGILL or SIGFPE, and a test that causes
          them is invalid

The end of stdin

    The Makefile append (^D) to the 'in' file and the console driver
    should interpret that as end of file (EOF).


Defining a test case:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

<csid>.in contains what goes into stdin for init (no ^D in it:
          see "The end of stdin")
<csid>.ok contains the expected output
<csid>.dir:
    - /sbin/init [required] the statically linked, non-relocatable test program
    - /sbin/src  [nice to have] the sources and Makefile that produces
                                /sbin/init


Developing your test:
   * the best approach is to copy t0.dir, t0.in and t0.ok and change
     src/init.c
   * you can build and run your own test on any CS Linux machine (only
     yours: see "Important" at the top). You don't need a working kernel
     to write your test which is why the test deadline is not tied to
     having t0 working and is much earlier than usual


For Makefile help:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    make help

To run tests:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    make clean test

To make the output less noisy:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    make -s clean test

Server view targets:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    make get_results    # gets and displays your results
    make get_tests      # gets tests from the server
    make get_summary    # shows results per test

To run one test
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    make -s t0.test               # run the test once
    make -s t0.loop               # run the test 10 times
    LOOP_LIMIT=6 make -s t0.loop  # run the test 6 times
    make -s t0.fail               # run the test until it fails

To run by hand
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    ./run_qemu t0

To run with gdb
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    ./debug_qemu t0

       Then follow the instructions on the screen

To type input for a test by hand
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    ./console_qemu t0

       Your keyboard is the console: each key goes straight to stdin
       (Console::getch), with no echo and no line editing. ^d ends the
       input: read returns 0 from then on. The kernel's debug output and
       the console's output both print to the terminal. ^c terminates QEMU.

To run with the QEMU monitor
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    ./monitor_qemu t0

       Like run_qemu, but ^a c switches the terminal between the debug
       output and the QEMU monitor (info registers, info mem, x/..., quit).
       The console reads <test>.in, like run_qemu. ^c doesn't terminate
       QEMU here, ^a x does.

Generated files
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

For each test, a set of files will be generated:

    <test>.debug        the debug output
    <test>.time         how long it took to run the test
    <test>.failure      failure information in case of failure
    <test>.out          the lines that start with *** from the raw output
    <test>.diff         the difference between <test>.out and <test>.ok
    <test>.result       pass or fail
    <test>.data         the ext2 disk image
    build/<test>.console what the console reads: <test>.in, then ^D


